-- Q1: medium / L3 — Two-class method effect on basic EPS with participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greystone Corp. has 500,000 common shares outstanding and net income of $1,000,000. It also has 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that share equally per share with common stock in dividends after receiving a $3 per share base dividend ($150,000 total). Under the two-class method, how does the participating preferred stock most likely affect basic EPS compared to simply deducting the base preferred dividend?', '["Basic EPS increases because preferred dividends are added back to the numerator", "Basic EPS is unchanged because participating preferred stock is excluded from the denominator", "Basic EPS decreases because undistributed earnings must be allocated between common and participating preferred shareholders", "Basic EPS decreases because participating preferred shares are added to the weighted-average denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, the two-class method requires allocating undistributed earnings between common stock and participating securities based on their respective dividend rights. This reduces the earnings attributable to common shareholders, lowering basic EPS below what it would be if only the base preferred dividend were deducted. Choice D is incorrect because the two-class method adjusts the numerator through earnings allocation, not by adding preferred shares to the denominator.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Mid-period conversion of convertible bonds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hemlock Industries has $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds outstanding on January 1, 2025. Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 common shares. On July 1, 2025, all bonds are converted. Hemlock has 200,000 common shares before conversion and a tax rate of 25%. For diluted EPS, how are the converted bonds treated?', '["The bonds are included under the if-converted method for the full year because conversion is assumed at the beginning of the period", "The if-converted method applies from January 1 to July 1, and the resulting shares are included as actual outstanding shares from July 1 to December 31", "The bonds are excluded entirely from diluted EPS because actual conversion occurred during the period", "The resulting 25,000 shares are included in both basic and diluted EPS for the full year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible securities are actually converted during the period, the if-converted method applies from the beginning of the period to the conversion date, and the resulting shares enter the weighted-average as outstanding common shares from the conversion date forward. Choice A is incorrect because the full-year if-converted assumption applies only when bonds remain outstanding for the entire period, not when mid-period conversion occurs.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Simple capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What defines a simple capital structure for EPS reporting purposes under U.S. GAAP?', '["The entity has only common stock outstanding with no potentially dilutive securities", "The entity has fewer than 500 shareholders of record", "The entity reports net income below $1,000,000", "The entity has only one class of equity securities and no convertible debt"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has no potentially dilutive securities such as convertible bonds, stock options, or warrants. Entities with simple capital structures present only basic EPS. Choice D is incorrect because having one class of equity does not preclude the existence of other potentially dilutive securities like options or warrants that would make the structure complex.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluate unvested restricted stock with non-forfeitable dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Technologies grants 20,000 shares of unvested restricted stock with non-forfeitable dividend rights to executives in 2025. Meridian has 400,000 common shares and net income of $2,000,000. The CFO argues that because the restricted shares are unvested, they should be excluded from basic EPS entirely. Evaluate the CFO''s position and determine the correct treatment under ASC 260.', '["The CFO is correct; unvested restricted stock is always excluded from both basic and diluted EPS until it vests", "The CFO is correct; restricted stock enters basic EPS only upon vesting and is irrelevant before that date", "The CFO is partially correct; restricted stock is excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method", "The CFO is incorrect; unvested restricted stock with non-forfeitable dividend rights is a participating security and must be included in basic EPS under the two-class method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, unvested share-based payment awards with non-forfeitable rights to dividends are participating securities. The two-class method requires allocating undistributed earnings to these awards when computing basic EPS, meaning they cannot be excluded. Choice C is incorrect because the treasury stock method applies to options and warrants, not to participating securities with non-forfeitable dividend rights.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Share buyback effect on EPS trend analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinebrook Holdings had 1,000,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and repurchased 200,000 shares on October 1, 2025. Net income was $4,500,000 in both 2024 and 2025, and no shares were repurchased in 2024. How does the 2025 share repurchase most likely affect year-over-year EPS comparisons?', '["Basic EPS remains unchanged because net income did not change between years", "Basic EPS increases because the weighted-average share count decreases while net income remains constant", "Basic EPS decreases because treasury stock repurchases reduce total stockholders'' equity", "Basic EPS is unaffected because repurchased shares remain in the denominator until retired"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS uses weighted-average shares outstanding. The October 1 repurchase reduces 2025 weighted-average shares from 1,000,000 to 950,000 (1,000,000 x 9/12 + 800,000 x 3/12), increasing EPS from $4.50 to approximately $4.74 while net income is unchanged. Choice A is incorrect because even with constant net income, the lower denominator causes EPS to rise, which can overstate apparent profitability improvement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Post-period stock split retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverwood Corp. has 300,000 shares outstanding during all of 2025. On February 15, 2026, before the 2025 financial statements are issued, the board declares a 2-for-1 stock split. What weighted-average share count should Silverwood report for 2025 basic EPS?', '["600,000 shares, reflecting the retroactive adjustment for the stock split", "300,000 shares, since the split occurred after the 2025 reporting period", "450,000 shares, weighted for the portion of 2026 elapsed before issuance", "600,000 shares, but disclosed only in the footnotes rather than in the EPS calculation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in all periods presented. The weighted-average shares are restated to 600,000 for 2025 EPS. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive treatment of stock splits regardless of whether the split occurs in the subsequent period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — EPS presentation requirements on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which EPS figures must public companies present on the face of the income statement?', '["Only basic EPS for net income", "Only diluted EPS for net income and continuing operations", "Basic and diluted EPS for both income from continuing operations and net income", "Basic EPS for continuing operations and diluted EPS for net income only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities must present both basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and for net income. If discontinued operations exist, per-share amounts may appear on the face or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because dual presentation of both basic and diluted EPS is required for entities with complex capital structures.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Bond premium amortization effect on diluted EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverford Inc. has $2,000,000 of 8% convertible bonds issued at a premium, with annual premium amortization of $20,000 reducing interest expense. The tax rate is 25%. When computing diluted EPS under the if-converted method, how does the bond premium amortization most likely affect the numerator adjustment?', '["Premium amortization increases the interest add-back because it represents additional borrowing cost", "Premium amortization has no effect on the numerator because only the stated coupon rate is used", "Premium amortization is excluded because it is a noncash item similar to depreciation", "Premium amortization reduces the interest add-back because it decreases the interest expense recognized on the income statement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted numerator adjustment adds back actual interest expense recognized, net of tax. Because premium amortization reduces interest expense below the cash coupon amount ($160,000 − $20,000 = $140,000), the after-tax add-back is $105,000 rather than $120,000. Choice A is incorrect because premium amortization decreases recognized interest expense under the effective interest method, reducing the add-back.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Discontinued operations and per-share amounts
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarcliff Industries reports income from continuing operations of $750,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $200,000, resulting in net income of $550,000. The company has 100,000 weighted-average shares and no preferred stock. How does the discontinued operations loss affect the EPS presentation?', '["A separate per-share amount of ($2.00) for discontinued operations must be disclosed on the face of the income statement or in the notes", "Discontinued operations are excluded from all per-share calculations under ASC 260", "The loss is reflected only in diluted EPS, not basic EPS", "Only net income EPS of $5.50 is reported, with no separate discontinued operations disclosure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities reporting discontinued operations must present per-share amounts for that line item either on the face of the income statement or in the notes. The per-share loss is $200,000 / 100,000 = $2.00. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically requires per-share disclosure for discontinued operations rather than excluding them from EPS presentation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Written put options and the reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Alder Creek Corp. has written put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $50 per share. The average market price during the year is $40 per share. Using the reverse treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the diluted EPS denominator?', '["No incremental shares because the put options are out of the money", "10,000 incremental shares because all shares under the put are added", "2,500 incremental shares", "12,500 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, the reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity issues enough shares at the average market price ($40) to generate proceeds to satisfy the put obligation ($500,000). This requires issuing 12,500 shares ($500,000 / $40). The net incremental shares are 12,500 issued minus 10,000 retired = 2,500. Choice A is incorrect because written puts are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the market price, not antidilutive.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L3 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred shares and EPS impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornwell Financial issues 5,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock at $100 per share, paying a 7% annual dividend. Under ASC 480, these shares are classified as liabilities. Thornwell has net income of $3,000,000, 500,000 common shares, and $200,000 in dividends on equity-classified preferred stock. Analyze how the mandatorily redeemable preferred dividends affect the basic EPS numerator.', '["The $35,000 dividend is subtracted from the numerator because it is a preferred dividend regardless of balance sheet classification", "The $35,000 is not subtracted from the numerator because the mandatorily redeemable shares are classified as liabilities, and the payments are treated as interest expense already deducted in net income", "The $35,000 is added back to the numerator because liability-classified preferred stock increases earnings available to common shareholders", "Both the $35,000 and the $200,000 are excluded from the numerator because all preferred dividends are already reflected in net income"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 480-10-25, mandatorily redeemable preferred shares are classified as liabilities, and their dividends are reclassified as interest expense in the income statement. Since this expense is already deducted in arriving at net income, no further numerator adjustment is needed for those shares. Choice A is incorrect because the EPS dividend deduction applies only to equity-classified preferred stock, not to instruments classified as liabilities under ASC 480.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Participating preferred allocation under two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Manufacturing has 200,000 common shares and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock. Net income is $750,000. Preferred stock receives a $2 per share base dividend ($100,000 total) and then participates equally per share with common stock in remaining earnings. Under the two-class method, what is basic EPS?', '["$3.75", "$3.25", "$3.00", "$2.60"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, undistributed earnings of $650,000 ($750,000 minus $100,000 base preferred dividend) must be allocated between common and participating preferred based on share counts. Common receives 200,000/250,000 x $650,000 = $520,000. Basic EPS = $520,000 / 200,000 = $2.60. Choice B ($3.25) is incorrect because it deducts only the base preferred dividend without allocating undistributed earnings to the participating preferred shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Nonpublic entities not required to present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which entities are required to present earnings per share on their financial statements?', '["All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements regardless of size", "Only public business entities with common stock or potential common stock traded on a public market", "All entities with annual revenues exceeding $10 million", "Only entities that have outstanding stock options or convertible securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public business entities with common stock or potential common stock that is publicly traded. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly limits the EPS requirement to public business entities, not all GAAP preparers.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Antidilutive control number — loss from continuing operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. reports a net loss from continuing operations of $300,000 and income from discontinued operations of $450,000, resulting in net income of $150,000. The company has convertible bonds that would otherwise be dilutive. Analyze how the loss from continuing operations affects the inclusion of potentially dilutive securities in the EPS calculation.', '["All potentially dilutive securities must be excluded from diluted EPS because the control number is a loss from continuing operations", "The convertible bonds are included in diluted EPS because overall net income is positive", "Only the discontinued operations per-share amount includes the dilutive securities", "The bonds are antidilutive only if their per-share impact exceeds the net loss per share"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations is the control number for determining dilution. When this amount is a loss, including any potentially dilutive securities would reduce the loss per share, which is antidilutive. All such securities must be excluded from diluted EPS. Choice B is incorrect because positive net income does not override the control number; the dilution test is based on continuing operations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Warrants treasury stock method calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. has 150,000 shares outstanding and 8,000 warrants with an exercise price of $30 per share. The average market price during 2025 is $48 per share. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the diluted EPS denominator?', '["8,000 shares", "5,000 shares", "3,000 shares", "2,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes exercise proceeds of 8,000 x $30 = $240,000 are used to repurchase shares at the average market price of $48, buying back 5,000 shares. The incremental shares are 8,000 minus 5,000 = 3,000. Choice A (8,000) is incorrect because it assumes all warrant shares are added without accounting for the assumed repurchase of shares with the exercise proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Ordering/sequencing of multiple dilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Holdings has basic EPS of $5.00 based on $1,500,000 net income and 300,000 shares. It has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares with no numerator effect; (2) $500,000 of 4% convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts to 20 shares, tax rate 25%); and (3) 10,000 shares of 6% convertible preferred stock ($100 par) each converting into 15,000 total common shares. Evaluate the correct ordering for testing dilution under ASC 260.', '["Test the security with the largest share increase first to maximize the denominator", "Rank from lowest to highest per-share impact: options first, then test convertible bonds and preferred by their respective per-share effects", "Test all three simultaneously by combining all numerator and denominator impacts at once", "Begin with the convertible preferred because preferred dividends were already deducted from basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are ranked from most dilutive (lowest per-share impact) to least dilutive. Stock options always rank first because they have zero numerator impact. Convertible bonds and preferred are ranked by their respective numerator increase divided by denominator increase. Choice C is incorrect because simultaneous inclusion could mask an antidilutive security; the sequential test ensures each is individually dilutive before inclusion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Interim EPS quarterly vs. year-to-date discrepancy
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mapleton Inc. reports quarterly EPS of $1.00, $1.20, $0.80, and $1.50 for Q1 through Q4 of 2025. The full-year EPS calculated directly is $4.40, but the sum of the four quarterly amounts is $4.50. How does this discrepancy most likely arise?', '["The discrepancy indicates an arithmetic error in the quarterly EPS calculations that must be corrected", "The company failed to retroactively adjust for a stock dividend during the year", "Year-to-date EPS should always equal the sum of quarterly EPS under ASC 260", "The discrepancy arises from changes in weighted-average shares outstanding each quarter, causing quarterly amounts not to sum to the annual figure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2 and ASC 270, quarterly EPS uses each quarter''s weighted-average shares, while annual EPS uses the full-year weighted-average. Because share counts change from issuances or repurchases, the sum of quarterly EPS will not necessarily equal annual EPS. Choice A is incorrect because this difference is an expected mathematical result of varying quarterly denominators, not a computational error.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Contingent shares with annualization test
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redfield Corp. has a contingent share agreement to issue 50,000 additional shares if cumulative net income over three years exceeds $9,000,000. At the end of Year 2, cumulative net income is $6,800,000. Year 2 net income alone is $3,600,000. For Year 2 diluted EPS, the controller must assess whether these contingent shares should be included. Evaluate the correct treatment under ASC 260.', '["Include all 50,000 shares because the company is on pace to exceed the target based on prior-year trends", "Exclude all 50,000 shares because the cumulative target has not yet been met at the end of Year 2", "Include all 50,000 shares because if Year 2 earnings were annualized for the remaining contingency period, the cumulative target would be exceeded", "Include a pro-rata portion of 37,778 shares based on the percentage of the earnings target achieved to date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if the conditions would be satisfied assuming current-period earnings continued for the remaining contingency period. Annualizing Year 2 earnings ($3,600,000) for Year 3 plus cumulative ($6,800,000) = $10,400,000, exceeding $9,000,000. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 260 requires all-or-nothing inclusion of contingent shares, not pro-rata allocation based on target completion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — RSUs effect on diluted EPS using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hargrove Corp. granted 12,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) to employees with a three-year service condition. At year-end, the average unrecognized compensation cost is $180,000, and the average market price is $45 per share. How do these unvested RSUs most likely affect diluted EPS under the treasury stock method?', '["All 12,000 RSU shares are added to the denominator because they will eventually vest", "The unrecognized compensation cost is treated as assumed proceeds, and the treasury stock method yields 8,000 incremental shares", "RSUs are excluded from diluted EPS until they vest and become outstanding common shares", "The RSUs reduce the EPS numerator by $180,000 to reflect future compensation expense"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, unvested RSUs without non-forfeitable dividend rights are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the unrecognized compensation cost ($180,000), used to repurchase $180,000 / $45 = 4,000 shares. Incremental shares = 12,000 minus 4,000 = 8,000. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method requires netting the assumed proceeds against the total RSU shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Complex multi-security diluted EPS computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgemont Enterprises reports net income of $1,200,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares. It has (1) 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $20 and an average market price of $40, (2) $400,000 of 5% convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts to 20 shares, tax rate 25%), and (3) 10,000 shares of convertible preferred stock with a $6 annual dividend, each converting to 3 common shares. Evaluate which securities are included and determine diluted EPS.', '["$5.00", "$5.42", "$5.56", "$5.70"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities ranked by per-share impact: options ($0/5,000 = $0), bonds ($15,000/8,000 = $1.875), preferred ($60,000/30,000 = $2.00). Testing sequentially from most dilutive: after options EPS = $5.56, after bonds = $5.42, after preferred = $5.00. All three are dilutive. Choice D ($5.70) is incorrect because it represents basic EPS and ignores all potentially dilutive securities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition for EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the treasury stock method used for in EPS calculations under ASC 260?', '["Determining the cost basis of treasury stock on the balance sheet", "Computing the gain or loss when treasury shares are reissued", "Calculating the weighted-average shares when stock dividends are declared", "Measuring the dilutive impact of stock options and warrants on diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method calculates the incremental dilutive shares from options and warrants by assuming exercise proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the average market price. Only the net incremental shares are added to the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method for EPS is unrelated to accounting for treasury stock transactions on the balance sheet.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Multiple classes of common stock and EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Holdings has two classes of common stock: 100,000 Class A shares with full dividend participation and 50,000 Class B shares limited to a $1.00 per share annual dividend. Net income is $600,000 and total dividends declared are $200,000, with Class B receiving $50,000 and Class A receiving $150,000. The CFO plans to report a single EPS figure using 150,000 total shares. Evaluate the appropriate treatment.', '["A single EPS figure using 150,000 total shares is acceptable because both classes are common stock", "EPS should be computed using only Class A shares because Class B has limited participation rights", "Separate EPS must be presented for each class of common stock based on their respective participation rights in earnings", "Class B shares should be excluded from EPS because their dividend rights differ from Class A"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60, when an entity has multiple classes of common stock with different participation rights, the two-class method requires separate EPS for each class based on their respective rights to dividends and undistributed earnings. Choice A is incorrect because combining classes with different participation rights into a single EPS figure would misrepresent the earnings attributable to each class of shareholders.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term "antidilutive" mean in the context of earnings per share under ASC 260?', '["A security that decreases EPS when included in the calculation", "A security that increases EPS or decreases loss per share when included in the calculation", "A security that has no effect on the EPS calculation", "A security whose exercise price equals the current market price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in diluted EPS would increase earnings per share or decrease loss per share. Antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS because including them would make reported performance appear more favorable. Choice A is incorrect because a security that decreases EPS is dilutive, not antidilutive, and would be included in diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Weighted-average with mid-year treasury stock purchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellwood Corp. has 180,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On September 1, 2025, Bellwood repurchases 24,000 shares as treasury stock. What is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for 2025?', '["172,000 shares", "180,000 shares", "156,000 shares", "168,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares account for the time period shares are outstanding. Bellwood had 180,000 shares for 8 months and 156,000 shares for 4 months: (180,000 x 8/12) + (156,000 x 4/12) = 120,000 + 52,000 = 172,000. Choice C (156,000) is incorrect because it assumes the repurchase occurred at the beginning of the year rather than weighting for the September 1 date.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted numerator adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashfield Corp. reports net income of $900,000 with 250,000 common shares outstanding. It has 5,000 shares of 8% convertible preferred stock ($100 par value), each convertible into 6 common shares. Under the if-converted method, what is the adjusted numerator for computing diluted EPS?', '["$860,000", "$870,000", "$940,000", "$900,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back the preferred dividends (5,000 x $100 x 8% = $40,000) that were subtracted from the basic EPS numerator. The adjusted numerator equals net income ($900,000) because the dividend deduction is restored. Choice A ($860,000) is incorrect because it reflects the basic EPS numerator without the add-back required under the if-converted method.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Control number identification
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what amount serves as the "control number" for determining whether a potentially dilutive security is dilutive or antidilutive?', '["Net income available to common shareholders", "Total comprehensive income for the reporting period", "Income from continuing operations available to common shareholders", "Operating income before nonoperating items"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations available to common shareholders serves as the control number for testing dilution. A security is antidilutive if its inclusion would increase this per-share amount or decrease a loss per share. Choice A is incorrect because net income includes discontinued operations, which are excluded from the control number used for dilution testing under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — ASC 260 as authoritative guidance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which Accounting Standards Codification topic provides the authoritative guidance on earnings per share?', '["ASC 260", "ASC 220", "ASC 280", "ASC 320"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 260, Earnings Per Share, provides the comprehensive guidance on computing and presenting basic and diluted EPS for public business entities. It covers the weighted-average share calculations, treasury stock method, if-converted method, and presentation requirements. Choice C (ASC 280) is incorrect because it addresses Segment Reporting, not earnings per share calculations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Diluted EPS definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does diluted earnings per share represent under ASC 260?', '["Earnings per share calculated using only outstanding common shares at year-end", "Earnings per share after deducting all preferred dividends from net income", "Earnings per share assuming the maximum number of shares that could ever be issued", "Earnings per share reflecting the potential dilution from securities that could result in common share issuance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, diluted EPS reflects the potential dilution that would occur if securities such as options, warrants, and convertible instruments were exercised or converted into common stock. Only dilutive securities are included; antidilutive ones are excluded. Choice C is incorrect because diluted EPS does not assume maximum possible issuance; it includes only those securities that would actually reduce EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Assumed proceeds include unrecognized compensation cost
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. has 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $25 per share. The average market price during 2025 is $50. The average unrecognized compensation cost attributable to future services is $5 per option. Under the treasury stock method, what are the total assumed proceeds used to calculate the share repurchase?', '["$500,000, using only the exercise price", "$600,000, including the exercise price and unrecognized compensation cost", "$400,000, using the exercise price minus the intrinsic value", "$700,000, including the exercise price and a tax benefit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds in the treasury stock method include the exercise price ($500,000) plus the average unrecognized compensation cost attributable to future service ($100,000 = 20,000 x $5), totaling $600,000. These proceeds are assumed used to repurchase shares at the average market price. Choice A ($500,000) is incorrect because it omits the unrecognized compensation cost that ASC 260 requires as part of assumed proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Stock dividend retroactive adjustment for full year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarpoint Industries has 400,000 shares outstanding for all of 2025 and declares a 5% stock dividend on December 15, 2025, distributable on January 20, 2026. When reporting full-year 2025 EPS, what weighted-average share count should Cedarpoint use?', '["400,000 shares because the stock dividend has not yet been distributed", "401,667 shares, weighting the dividend shares from December 15 forward", "420,000 shares, retroactively adjusted for the full year", "400,000 shares for basic EPS and 420,000 shares for diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are treated retroactively as if they occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented, regardless of the declaration or distribution date. The 5% stock dividend adds 20,000 shares (400,000 x 5%), restating the weighted-average to 420,000 for all periods. Choice A is incorrect because retroactive adjustment applies upon declaration, not distribution of the shares.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
